From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fardjad Davari <fardjad.davari2@jumbo.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 14:48:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add delegation efficiency measurement

X-Jcode-Patch-Intent: Measure and report delegation guard efficiency with content-free persistence and counterfactual net savings
X-Jcode-Patch-Kind: personal-feature
X-Jcode-Patch-Depends-On: 1011-personal-feature-add-hook-context-and-post-tool-transform.patch

Patch intent:
Add runtime measurement events, local plugin persistence, evidence-preserving
analysis, retention, and content-free reporting for delegation efficiency. When
the delegation guard intercepts an oversized tool output, it replaces the full
result with a short notice and the coordinator may delegate inspection to a
worker. The counterfactual model compares two scenarios for the same tool
invocation:

  Without the guard: the full original tool output would have been sent to
  the coordinator as input tokens. The estimated baseline cost is the original
  output token count multiplied by the guard event immutable input-rate
  snapshot, priced as estimated USD micros.

  With the guard: three communication components carry tokens instead. The
  guard notice to coordinator is the short replacement text. The
  coordinator-to-worker spawn is the task description sent to start the
  worker. The worker return or read to coordinator is the accumulated output
  the worker produces. Each component is priced independently using its own
  immutable input-rate snapshot.

  Net savings = baseline cost minus the sum of the three actual component
  costs. Each term uses integer floor division by 1,000,000. Zero-token
  events are valid. Missing required events, token counts, rates, or
  snapshots remain unavailable rather than displayed as zero.

A signed single-use delegation token is embedded in the guard nudge text and
validated by the worker spawn to link the guard event to the delegation with
exact, non-heuristic correlation.

Why it exists:
The runtime emits immutable scalar facts but must not own persistence or
reporting. The plugin keeps provider costs and source-derived estimates
separate, with private isolated state and no content storage. Strict exact
linkage, role, direction, immutable snapshots, and migration-safe schema
handling prevent unrelated communications, provider usage rows, duplicate
attempts, retries, or rate fallback from inflating savings.

Upstream integration points:
Consume the jcode.delegation-efficiency.v1 envelope emitted by the measurement
observer and preserve its guard, delegation, provider, communication, and
price-snapshot fields. The analysis relies on explicit delegation_id and
guard_event_id links, process_role, direction, communication token
measurements, and immutable event-time input-rate snapshots.

Update guidance:
Preserve immutable source facts, explicit linkage, process role, direction,
communication kind, evidence labels, privacy allowlists, separate token and
monetary units, captured price source/version/rates, and isolated state
resolution. Never reprice history from catalog drift or use output rates for
input communications.

Validation:
python3 -m unittest discover -s plugins/delegation-efficiency/tests -p 'test_plugin.py'
python3 -m unittest plugins/delegation-efficiency/tests/test_plugin.py -v
bash plugins/delegation-efficiency/install.sh <disposable-destination>
scripts/isolate_config.py -- bash scripts/test_install_patched_version_recipe.sh
